    "### 9.1 Analytical Solution — Normal Equation\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Purpose\n",
    "\n",
    "Calculate the slope and intercept directly using the Normal Equation,\n",
    "following the professor's workshop.\n",
    "\n",
    "Unlike gradient descent, this method does not require a learning rate\n",
    "or repeated parameter updates.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Data Used\n",
    "\n",
    "We reuse the variables created earlier in this notebook:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `X_train_scaled`: median income standardized using training-set statistics.\n",
    "- `y_train`: observed median house values in their original units.\n",
    "\n",
    "Only training observations are used to estimate the parameters.\n",
    "The test set remains excluded from fitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Model and Design Matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "Our model is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{y}_i = w x_i + b\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here, \\(x_i\\) is standardized median income, \\(w\\) is the slope,\n",
    "and \\(b\\) is the intercept.\n",
    "\n",
    "The design matrix contains two columns:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Standardized median income.\n",
    "2. Ones, allowing the model to estimate an intercept.\n",
    "\n",
    "We calculate:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\theta = (X_{\\text{design}}^T X_{\\text{design}})^{-1}\n",
    "X_{\\text{design}}^T y\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the predictor column comes first and the ones column second:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\theta =\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
    "w \\\\\n",
    "b\n",
    "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This follows the professor's explicit matrix-inverse implementation.\n",
    "It requires a full-rank design matrix; a constant predictor would\n",
    "prevent the inverse from being calculated.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Interpreting the Parameters\n",
    "\n",
    "On the standardized predictor scale:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `w` represents the predicted change in median house value for a\n",
    "  one-training-standard-deviation increase in median income.\n",
    "- `b` represents the predicted median house value when income equals\n",
    "  the training-set mean.\n",
    "\n",
    "For interpretation on the original income scale, we convert:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "w_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{w}{\\sigma_{\\text{train}}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "b_{\\text{original}} =\n",
    "b - \\frac{w\\mu_{\\text{train}}}{\\sigma_{\\text{train}}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "These are two equivalent representations of the same fitted line.\n",
    "The association does not establish causation."
   ]
  },
