    "### 9.6 Predict Median House Value for a New Income\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Purpose\n",
    "\n",
    "Use both fitted models to estimate median house value for a new\n",
    "median-income input, following the professor's prediction example.\n",
    "\n",
    "The professor supplies a new house size. Our adaptation supplies\n",
    "a new median income because that is our selected predictor.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### New Input\n",
    "\n",
    "We use:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{new median income} = 5.0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This value uses the original income scale of the CSV.\n",
    "It must not be interpreted as an income of five dollars.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Apply the Existing Transformation\n",
    "\n",
    "Both models were fitted using standardized income. Therefore, we\n",
    "transform the new input using the existing training scaler:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_{\\text{new, scaled}} =\n",
    "\\frac{x_{\\text{new}}-\\mu_{\\text{train}}}\n",
    "{\\sigma_{\\text{train}}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We call `scaler.transform()`, not `fit()` or `fit_transform()`.\n",
    "The training mean and standard deviation must remain unchanged.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Calculate Predictions\n",
    "\n",
    "For the Normal Equation model:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{y}_{NE} = w_{\\text{NE}}x_{\\text{new, scaled}} + b_{\\text{NE}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For the gradient-descent model:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{y}_{GD} = w_{\\text{GD}}x_{\\text{new, scaled}} + b_{\\text{GD}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Neither model is retrained. Predictions remain in the original\n",
    "median-house-value units because the target was not standardized.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Interpretation and Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "Both predictions should be nearly identical because the fitted\n",
    "parameters are nearly identical.\n",
    "\n",
    "The output estimates an area's median house value, not the price\n",
    "of a specific property. It is a point prediction, not a prediction\n",
    "interval or a guaranteed value.\n",
    "\n",
    "We also check whether the input is within the observed training\n",
    "income range. An input outside that range requires extrapolation.\n",
    "Being inside the range does not guarantee an accurate prediction."
   ]
